### Change Requests, review, rework, and checkpoint

A producer that hands off inside a managed workspace publishes a **Change
Request**: a revisioned record attached to the existing Task/Bug/Story, not a new
board element. It carries the branch, the base and candidate tree OIDs, a binary
patch resource with its SHA-256, the changed-path list, and an explicit
`repository_delta` of `present` or `empty`.

The candidate is snapshotted through a temporary alternate Git index, so tracked
changes, deletions, and untracked non-ignored files are all captured **without
touching the managed worktree's own index**. Every `.task-board/**` entry is then
restored from the base tree, which is why a `changed_paths` list can never
contain a board path — the authoritative board is the control root, and a
worktree's `.task-board` copy is a checkout artifact. Paths dropped by that rule
are recorded as `dropped_board_paths` and shown by `worktree status` and in the
reviewer's prompt. A non-empty list is a diagnostic, usually a `git add -A` in
the child, not a failure.

Construction runs for **every** producer completion in a managed workspace,
whether or not the run carries a delivery goal. Managed workspace activation
turns on role, task class, containing Story and local board alone, so an ordinary
`task-board spawn TASK-... --role developer --background` gets an isolated
worktree — and the half of the feature that makes that worktree reviewable
activates on exactly the same conditions.

It runs **after** the spawn completion artifact guard, and on the same two facts:
the board reached the run's end status, and the run attached a new or updated
task-scoped outcome artifact. A run that attached nothing is reverted to its
producer status by that guard and leaves no Change Request at all. A read that
FAILS is reported as a failure and never as "did not hand off".

A workspace-claiming producer that publishes nothing says why, on the run's event
log and in a board note: `handoff_unsatisfied`, with the detail — the board is
not at the end status, or no task-scoped outcome artifact was attached.

The other two reasons are recorded nowhere, on one rule: a reason that is true of
every run of its class says nothing about the run in front of you, and repeating
it on every completion buries the case the reporting exists for. An un-isolated
run skips with `no_managed_workspace`, which is true of every un-isolated run. A
reviewer-archetype run skips with `not_a_producer`, which is true of every
reviewer run — a reviewer judges a candidate rather than publishing one, so
noting it would append a line describing correct behaviour to the element's
progress on every single review cycle.

`repository_delta=empty` means the producer changed no repository file. It is an
attribute, never a state and never terminal: the Change Request is `ready` like
any other, a reviewer **is** spawned, and the injected prompt says in plain terms
that nothing changed and lists the outcome resources that were produced. Deciding
whether that was correct is a judgement about intent versus delivery, which is
what the reviewer archetype is for.

**The kind is derived from the board, never declared.** A Change Request is
`task_delta` or `story_final`, and the second is what `worktree integrate`
accepts as the Story's integration unit. Publication asks the board whether
completing this leaf would also close its Story — the same question
`worktree checkpoint` asks to REFUSE that leaf, through the same call — so the
leaf one command declines is exactly the leaf the other treats as the Story's
unit. Every indeterminate answer refuses the publication: no board access, an
unreadable board, or an element the board does not contain means the question was
never answered, and answering `task_delta` on a failed read would leave the Story
permanently unintegrable with nothing recording why.

**The base follows the kind.** A `task_delta` is measured from the Story branch
tip, where earlier leaves already checkpointed. A `story_final` is measured from
the branch's **trunk fork point**, so its patch covers trunk to the complete
Story candidate tree. It is deliberately not current trunk: the candidate tree is
a full snapshot, so diffing it against a trunk that moved after the fork would
present every concurrent landing as a reversal — which is exactly what the
re-parenting three-way merge exists to prevent, one layer further on.

**The producer runs the landing suite and records tree-bound evidence.** When
`spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` is configured, publication runs it
in the Story worktree and records `validation` keyed to the published candidate
tree. The suite runs against the WORKTREE while the evidence claims a TREE, so
the candidate is recomputed afterwards and compared; a suite that moved the
worktree — a build that drops artifacts, a codegen step, a formatter — yields
**no** evidence rather than evidence keyed to a tree it never saw. A failing
suite is recorded with its real exit status rather than suppressed, and a command
that could not run at all is never recorded as a pass. See
[how the evidence binds](#validation-binds-to-the-tree-that-lands).

Before the **final unresolved leaf's** producer starts, task-board refreshes the
clean Story checkpoint branch onto current trunk, so that producer works and
validates against the tree that will actually land. It refreshes nothing else: a
non-final leaf's candidate is a delta on the branch tip, and moving the fork point
under it would rewrite the base of work already reviewed. The refresh never fails
a spawn and never forces anything. A conflict aborts and leaves the branch exactly
as it was, routing to same-branch rework; a workspace holding uncommitted work is
skipped entirely, because a rebase under uncommitted work is the operation that
destroys work existing nowhere else. Both outcomes are reported on the element and
leave the recorded base untouched; only a successful refresh records the new
reviewed trunk OID.

Reviewer spawn recomputes the candidate first. A worktree that moved marks the
revision `stale` and refuses the spawn: task-board never reviews a moving
candidate.

The reviewer records exactly one verdict. Acceptance is a named mutation:

```bash
task-board m 'accept_cr(<ELEMENT-ID>, revision=<N>, evidence=<ELEMENT-ID>_review-verdict.md)'
```

Only the reviewer run that was handed the revision may run it. `accept_cr`
resolves the caller's tracked run, loads its manifest, and requires a
reviewer-archetype run bound to this element and this revision; an untracked
caller, a producer run, a reviewer bound to another element or another revision,
and a manifest that could not be read are all refused with
`change_request_acceptance_unauthorized`.

The `evidence` must also be **this run's own**: a task-scoped outcome resource
that did not exist when the reviewer launched, or whose content changed since.
The producer's `_results.md` sits on the element before the reviewer is spawned,
and naming it would record "reviewer run X accepted revision N with evidence E"
where E is the work under review rather than a judgement of it. Unchanged,
unproven (a legacy manifest with names but no digests), and unreadable evidence
are all refused with `change_request_evidence_missing`.

Acceptance is an attestation, and
`worktree checkpoint` treats the accepted record as its sole authorization to
close the leaf with `commit_ack=scope_committed` — a self-minted acceptance would
put a commit acknowledgement behind a review that never happened.

It sets the Change Request to `accepted`, stamps the reviewer run, and parks the
element at `to-review` — the **accepted handoff**, not `done`. For a
`story_final` revision it also records the **reviewed trunk OID**, which is the
record's own base: that is the trunk the reviewer's diff was taken against, and
integration reads it twice — the control root's `HEAD` must equal it, and the
paths trunk advanced since it decide whether the acceptance survives. The value
is **checked before it is recorded**: the base must be contained in trunk's
history, and a base that is not, or a repository that could not answer, refuses
the acceptance rather than writing an unverified claim into a field two gates
trust. A `task_delta` records no reviewed trunk OID at all — its base is a Story
branch tip, which is not a trunk commit and never becomes one. Accepted work never
stays in `reviewing`: a reviewer run that ends at bare `reviewing` with no
accepted Change Request is still cancelled and respawned, and the accepted branch
is derived from the record rather than from the status. `accept_cr` takes no
acknowledgement argument. `changes_requested` routes to `to-dev` or `analysis`
and the next producer publishes revision N+1 on the same branch and worktree;
prior revisions stay readable as immutable evidence. Stop-The-Line is unchanged.

A reviewer that is cancelled and respawned keeps its Change Request binding. The
successor inherits the revision, the CR id and the diff digest along with the
Story workspace and the prompt, because the prompt it reuses tells it to run
`accept_cr` on that revision — a successor handed no revision is the exact shape
`accept_cr` refuses, so the recovery ladder would run to its attempt limit and
park. A successor **rerouted to a different archetype** drops the binding
instead, and says so in its queue summary: a producer publishes revision N+1 by
reading the store, and it was never handed a candidate to accept.

The Orchestrator then closes a **non-final** leaf:

```bash
task-board worktree checkpoint <ELEMENT-ID>
```

That commits the accepted candidate tree as one internal commit on the Story
branch under a compare-and-swap on the branch tip, records it on the workspace,
and moves the leaf to `done`. Nothing reaches trunk: internal checkpoints only
delimit later Task diffs and make work recoverable, and the final Story squash
discards them. A `repository_delta=empty` revision creates no commit — its tree
would equal its parent's — and the leaf still reaches `done` by the same command.
A replayed checkpoint creates no second commit. A drifted worktree, a branch that
moved, and an unaccepted revision each refuse with a typed error and leave the
board and the branch exactly as they were.

**A checkpoint never closes the Story.** If the leaf is the Story's last open
child, moving it to `done` would promote the Story in the same mutation, and the
blanket `commit_ack=scope_committed` would then satisfy the version-control gate
for a Story whose branch has provably never reached trunk. That is refused with
`change_request_final_leaf_checkpoint`, naming
`task-board worktree integrate <STORY-ID>` — the step that lands the branch
before making the transition. The rule is about scope, not acknowledgement: it
applies whether or not `version_control` confirmation is enabled, and it runs
before the drift check, the commit, the record transition and the status change,
so the accepted revision survives for the integration path to use. A board that
cannot be read, or an element the board does not contain, refuses for the same
reason — the checkpoint's scope is then unknown rather than clear.

**Checkpoint the leaves of a Story in the order their candidates were published.**
A checkpoint asserts that the branch tip still equals the revision's recorded
base. If leaf B's producer completes before leaf A is checkpointed, both
revisions carry the same base; checkpointing A moves the tip, and B then refuses
permanently with `change_request_checkpoint_conflict`. The refusal is correct and
leaves the branch recoverable, but the recovery is **republish revision N+1**,
not retry — base refresh is not part of this contract.

Change Request persistence is **local-board only**. `accept_cr` and Change
Request construction both refuse a resolved remote board with
`worktree_isolation_requires_local_board` before any record is written, any diff
resource is uploaded, or any element field is mutated. There is no remote Change
Request path and none is emulated.

task-board spawn events RUN-260320-abc123
task-board spawn events RUN-260320-abc123 --cursor RUN-260320-abc123:5
task-board spawn watch RUN-260320-abc123 --cursor RUN-260320-abc123:5
task-board spawn observe RUN-260320-abc123 --cursor RUN-260320-abc123:5 --until terminal --format compact
task-board spawn wait RUN-260320-abc123
task-board spawn directives RUN-260320-abc123
task-board spawn directive RUN-260320-abc123 cancel
task-board spawn goal RUN-260320-abc123
```

Every valid explicit spawn prints these monitor commands automatically after queueing, seeded with cursor `RUN-ID:0` and bound to the same `task-board` executable / board context that launched the run.
In JSON mode the same contract is returned under `observation`:
`cursor`, `eventsCommand`, `watchCommand`, `waitCommand`, `runtime`, `delivery`, and `inSessionDelivery`.
`observation.runtime` reports the orchestrator session runtime (`codex-session`, `claude-session`, or `external`) and `observation.delivery` names its non-blocking completion channel (`codex-in-session`, `claude-background-wait`, or `external-watch`).

Use the printed `observe --until terminal --format compact` command as the
default single surface: it consumes events newer than its durable cursor and
appends terminal checklist/outcome handoff evidence without replaying earlier
history. Use `status` for a diagnostic snapshot, `events` for replay, `watch`
for follow mode, and `wait` only for a barrier that does not need event/handoff
detail. Do not stack these reads on the routine success path.
In JSON mode, `spawn watch` uses notification NDJSON framing only (one notification object per line, no trailing summary payload).

### Pattern C: Durable Directive Plane

- `spawn directive RUN-... <kind>` records a durable operator directive with acknowledgement state.
- `spawn directives RUN-...` replays the directive history for that run.
- `cancel` is applied immediately for queued runs and terminates the background runner/process group for running runs when the platform supports it.
- `pause_after_checkpoint`, `request_progress`, and `nudge` are cooperative signals. Current runtimes expose `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID`; the child may poll `task-board spawn directives "$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID"` at safe checkpoints.
- `restart` and `reroute_after_checkpoint` create successor `RUN-...` records instead of rewriting existing run history. Running reroute/pause requests are rejected when the runtime cannot checkpoint safely in place.
- Both directive commands print the current active goal header before directive details. New records persist the immutable goal ID/revision under which they were issued; legacy records print `issued_under_goal: unknown`.
- Restart and a reroute that preserves the current goal ID, revision, role binding, scope, kind, predicate, and intent remain owner-agent recovery actions. Goal-bound `cancel` and non-monotonic reroute are operator-authoritative controls and require a non-empty reason. A structured replacement validates its requested scope/kind/predicate against the successor role before it writes the directive or successor.

### Pattern D: Goal-Bound Delivery Pool

After plan approval and before the first producer launch or delivery status transition, start one independently tracked orchestrator owner:

```bash
task-board spawn STORY-260218-auth \
  --role orchestrator \
  --goal-scope TASK-260218-a,TASK-260218-b \
  --background \
  --agent codex \
  --model gpt-5.6-sol \
  --reasoning-effort max
```

The CLI resolves containers to an active dependency-closed task/bug pool,
derives `delivery_pool/accepted_done` from the normalized orchestrator role,
reserves the RUN/GOAL lineage, validates provider capability and the
primary-session launch plan, and only then permits normal launch side effects.
The Session Manager attaches the exact `run/RUN-*` owner, reconciles its native
goal, and submits the assignment only after the session's applied revision
matches the authoritative v2 head. Nested specialists receive role-derived
`role_handoff` or `reviewer_verdict` owners without replacing the parent
`delivery_pool` condition.

The authoritative state is the `board-goal-v2` `run/RUN-*` owner head and its
immutable revision history. `manifest.launch_goal`, `state.active_goal_ref`,
and run-local revision files are one-release `board-goal-v1` compatibility
mirrors written with the v2 promotion. Failed native activation leaves the
prior head active; an unbound head is `pending-unbound` until the provider
acknowledges that exact revision.

Parent and run owner heads are isolated manager slots. The spawned provider
environment strips parent manager/app-server endpoints, auth indirections and
their token values, and parent provider session markers. Persisted caller-plane
fields are decode-only compatibility data; new launches issue no caller RPC.
One-release restoration remains only for an active legacy manifest that
already contains an exact caller snapshot.

At every checkpoint:

```bash
task-board spawn goal "$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID"
task-board spawn directives "$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID"
```

If a nested target resolves outside the current pool, `spawn` returns non-zero `goal_scope_mismatch` before assignment, prompt, manifest, provider, or child-process effects. The payload classifies missing leaves as `adjacent`, `outside`, or `mixed` and includes the exact optimistic update command. Run that command only when the work is genuinely required, then retry the spawn.

Nested spawn also fails closed on its declared parent execution context. While
a legacy already-running Claude owner has `successor_required`, the command
returns typed `goal_provider_binding_pending` with `side_effects=false`; new
goal-bound Claude owners start directly in manager-held PTYs. If the declared
parent manifest, state, or active revision cannot be loaded, the command
returns `goal_parent_context_unavailable` rather than treating the caller as
non-goal.

Owner-agent changes are monotonic: create with `--scope`, expand with `--add-scope`, refresh only the non-authoritative intent, and supply `--if-revision` for updates. Upsert responses expose `mutation.previous_revision` as `0` on creation, the prior active revision on a new immutable revision, and the current revision on an idempotent replay. Restart and same-semantics reroute retain that latest revision under a rebound successor owner. Scope removal, structural replacement, weakening, kind changes, and cancellation cross the operator directive boundary and create durable cancelled/superseded lineage rather than successful delivery.

For a legacy already-running Claude run, `successor_required` retains its
one-release safe-checkpoint behavior. New manager-owned sessions do not use
that split path.

Provider usage/budget limits, transient launch or transport failures, nonzero goal-owner exits, unmet producer handoff status/evidence, missing reviewer branches, and unmet delivery acceptance persist a structured recovery cause and automatically queue a successor. A goal-bound predecessor rebinds the same active goal revision; a context-only predecessor drops its predecessor-owned writable child and managed envelope; a run owned by no plane gets an ordinary prompt-mode successor. The predecessor is superseded by recovery, never successful delivery.

Rate/usage-limit recovery preserves the real provider error on the run and
element, but never parses or trusts a reset timestamp in that text. The runtime
uses bounded local exponential backoff, then probes availability by launching
the same persisted model. Recovery lineage is capped at three successors; when
the cap is reached, the last run becomes terminal `recovery_parked` and the
element returns to its actionable queue (`to-review` for review work, `to-dev`
for development) with an operator-action note instead of remaining silently
parked in an active status. A non-recoverable provider-capability failure does
not create a successor, but follows the same board-visible treatment: its real
provider cause is recorded on the terminal run and element, and the element is
routed back to the actionable role queue. A provider owner-session binding
rotation preserves the existing recovery lineage and does not spend one of
those attempts.

Failed terminalization uses a durable element-routing outbox. The run records
the exact operator message and pending routing before it becomes terminal. If
the board write fails, status/wait/follow repair retries the same routing after
the board recovers and records a routed timestamp only after both the note and
role-specific status mutation succeed. Note delivery is acknowledged separately,
so retrying a failed status mutation does not append the same operator note
again. This covers recovery preparation,
attempt-cap parking, provider capability, heartbeat expiry, and generic failed
terminal paths. Repair isolates failures per run: one deleted or otherwise
unroutable element cannot abort the rest of the sweep, block slot claims, or
break waits for unrelated runs. Runtime-owned exponential backoff spaces
routing attempts. Transient board failures stay pending without consuming the
terminal bound. Permanence requires structured local `board.ErrNotFound` or a
typed remote mutation failure decoded from the server's HTTP 200 `ok:false`
envelope with the exact `element <ID>: not found` message shape. Remote 404/5xx
responses and incidental "not found" prose remain retryable. Three consecutive
permanent failures exhaust, and that state is preserved on run status with the
exact last error.
Operator cancellation stages the same outbox with the supplied reason and
returns the element to its role-specific actionable queue.

Stale-run repair follows that same no-silence rule. When a tracked runner dies
and its heartbeat expires, the run terminates with `heartbeat_expired`; the
element records the exact cause and returns from the run's active start status
to `to-review` for reviewer work or `to-dev` for development. This path parks
for explicit orchestration rather than guessing whether a killed runner is safe
to resume.

If successor log preparation or runner startup fails, its manifest and state
remain non-terminal `queued` with a persisted deferred-start cause, retry time,
and scheduler lease. Status, wait, and follow observers claim an eligible or
expired lease under the scheduler lock, retry that same successor outside the
lock, and clear the deferred marker only after runner start/heartbeat. The
predecessor may already be superseded, but the same immutable goal revision
still has exactly one retryable owner instead of two terminal runs.

Provider `blocked` and a role that exits at board `blocked` use the same
Stop-The-Line gate. Board `blocked` plus a new or updated task-scoped evidence
packet terminates the run with `completion_reason=stop_boundary`; reviewer runs
also persist the `stop_the_line` verdict. This branch never satisfies delivery
acceptance. Without the status/evidence pair, the boundary claim is recoverable
and the same revision is queued again.

Producer provider exit is not handoff success. A `role_handoff` run must reach its immutable role end status and add or update a task-scoped outcome artifact relative to its launch snapshot. Zero exit while the board remains in `analysis`/`development`, or at the handoff status without evidence, queues autonomous same-revision recovery.

After attaching the task-scoped outcome, producers should use
`task-board handoff TASK-ID --role ROLE`. The command resolves the configured
role end status, verifies every checklist item and an actual stored task-scoped
outcome, then performs the evidence decision and ordinary status transition
under one mutation lock. It reports all missing checklist/outcome evidence and
leaves status unchanged when the gate fails. Spawn-log artifacts do not count as
producer outcomes, and the runtime still enforces its launch-relative outcome
snapshot independently.

Delivery success is not provider completion alone. Reviewer goals persist exactly one evidence-backed branch derived from board state: `accepted`, `changes_requested`, or `stop_the_line`; an exit while still `reviewing` triggers recovery. `required`/`light` leaves need board `done`, task-scoped producer outcome evidence, and a later reviewer whose persisted branch is `accepted`. `none` leaves need `done`, producer outcome evidence, and the owner’s completion-time evidence check.

### Pattern E: Delivery Boundary (Watcher vs In-Session)

- `spawn events`, `spawn watch`, and `spawn observe` are external watcher surfaces built on persisted run events.
- They can notify a human/operator and feed TUI/remote push adapters.
- They do **not** inject inbound text into the currently active orchestrator session by default.
